1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,172
Hi everyone, and welcome to Linear Algebra at WenQuest.

2
00:00:04,572 --> 00:00:08,013
This is Chapter 14, Section 2, the spectral theorem.

3
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:12,044
Let's start with a problem from the shop floor.

4
00:00:12,444 --> 00:00:15,703
A robot arm holds a grinding wheel, or a deburring tool.

5
00:00:15,723 --> 00:00:18,689
The tool presses on the part, the part pushes back,

6
00:00:18,709 --> 00:00:21,149
and the tip of the arm gives way a little.

7
00:00:21,549 --> 00:00:25,711
That little bit can be a few tenths of a millimetre of error on the finished part.

8
00:00:26,111 --> 00:00:29,985
So an engineer wants to know: in which direction is the tip softest,

9
00:00:30,005 --> 00:00:33,136
in which is it stiffest, and how big is the difference?

10
00:00:33,536 --> 00:00:37,679
And a more practical question: if you push in some arbitrary direction,

11
00:00:37,699 --> 00:00:39,496
where does the tip actually go?

12
00:00:39,896 --> 00:00:44,164
In the last section we proved that the tip stiffness matrix is symmetric,

13
00:00:44,184 --> 00:00:48,041
and that the eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix are perpendicular.

14
00:00:48,442 --> 00:00:50,995
If we can always find a full set of them,

15
00:00:51,015 --> 00:00:55,639
the stiffness matrix is just a few independent springs along perpendicular

16
00:00:55,659 --> 00:00:56,329
directions.

17
00:00:56,729 --> 00:01:00,271
Today's result, the spectral theorem, guarantees exactly that.

18
00:01:01,791 --> 00:01:06,661
The spectral theorem says: let A be a real symmetric matrix of size n by n.

19
00:01:07,061 --> 00:01:12,397
Then there is an orthogonal matrix Q and a real diagonal matrix Lambda such

20
00:01:12,417 --> 00:01:15,753
that A equals Q times Lambda times Q transpose.

21
00:01:16,153 --> 00:01:19,826
Orthogonal means Q transpose times Q is the identity.

22
00:01:19,846 --> 00:01:22,822
The columns are perpendicular unit vectors.

23
00:01:23,222 --> 00:01:27,362
The columns of Q are an orthonormal set of eigenvectors of A,

24
00:01:27,382 --> 00:01:31,727
and the diagonal entries of Lambda are the matching eigenvalues.

25
00:01:32,127 --> 00:01:33,795
The converse is true too.

26
00:01:33,815 --> 00:01:38,454
Any matrix of the form Q Lambda Q transpose equals its own transpose,

27
00:01:38,474 --> 00:01:39,737
so it is symmetric.

28
00:01:40,137 --> 00:01:41,056
In other words,

29
00:01:41,076 --> 00:01:45,877
being real symmetric and being diagonalizable by an orthogonal matrix are the

30
00:01:45,897 --> 00:01:46,566
same thing.

31
00:01:48,086 --> 00:01:51,713
The proof is by induction. Let's look only at the key step.

32
00:01:52,113 --> 00:01:57,768
The characteristic polynomial always has a root, so take an eigenvalue λ₁ of A.

33
00:01:57,788 --> 00:02:02,724
We showed last time it must be real. Take a real unit eigenvector q₁,

34
00:02:02,744 --> 00:02:05,166
extend it to an orthonormal basis,

35
00:02:05,186 --> 00:02:08,542
and put the basis into an orthogonal matrix Q₁.

36
00:02:08,942 --> 00:02:12,331
Now change coordinates with Q₁ and see what A becomes.

37
00:02:12,731 --> 00:02:17,099
The lower-left block is UᵀAq₁, which is λ₁Uᵀq₁.

38
00:02:17,119 --> 00:02:23,074
The columns of U are perpendicular to q₁, so this block is zero.

39
00:02:23,474 --> 00:02:24,743
Here is the key point.

40
00:02:24,763 --> 00:02:28,434
The upper-right block is the transpose of the lower-left block,

41
00:02:28,454 --> 00:02:29,723
so it is zero as well.

42
00:02:30,123 --> 00:02:33,789
For a general matrix this step clears only the first column.

43
00:02:33,809 --> 00:02:37,290
For a symmetric matrix the first row is cleared for free.

44
00:02:37,690 --> 00:02:41,286
What is left, B, is still symmetric and one size smaller.

45
00:02:41,306 --> 00:02:44,839
Repeat the same step on B, peel off one layer at a time,

46
00:02:44,859 --> 00:02:47,059
and you end with a diagonal matrix.

47
00:02:48,579 --> 00:02:51,068
The theorem has some immediate consequences.

48
00:02:51,468 --> 00:02:54,941
First, a symmetric matrix can always be diagonalized.

49
00:02:54,961 --> 00:02:59,818
Every eigenvalue has as many independent eigenvectors as its multiplicity,

50
00:02:59,838 --> 00:03:04,233
so unlike the shear matrix in the last chapter, we never run short.

51
00:03:04,633 --> 00:03:07,588
Second, the diagonalizing matrix is orthogonal.

52
00:03:07,608 --> 00:03:11,701
Its inverse is just its transpose, so there is nothing to invert.

53
00:03:12,101 --> 00:03:14,376
You will often see this stated as:

54
00:03:14,396 --> 00:03:19,302
every real symmetric matrix is orthogonally similar to a diagonal matrix.

55
00:03:19,702 --> 00:03:23,391
In code, always use numpy's eigh for symmetric matrices.

56
00:03:23,411 --> 00:03:28,160
It returns the eigenvalues in increasing order and an orthonormal set of

57
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:29,021
eigenvectors.

58
00:03:30,541 --> 00:03:34,583
The theorem is easier to trust once you have done one by hand.

59
00:03:34,603 --> 00:03:37,465
Take the simplest case, A equals (2 1; 1 2).

60
00:03:37,865 --> 00:03:43,379
Step one, the eigenvalues. The determinant of A minus λ I is two minus λ,

61
00:03:43,399 --> 00:03:48,609
squared, minus one, which factors as λ minus one times λ minus three.

62
00:03:48,629 --> 00:03:50,959
So the eigenvalues are 1 and 3.

63
00:03:51,359 --> 00:03:54,568
Step two, the eigenvectors. For λ equals 1,

64
00:03:54,588 --> 00:03:59,825
solve A minus I times x equals zero and you get the direction (1, −1).

65
00:03:59,845 --> 00:04:02,079
For λ equals 3 you get (1, 1).

66
00:04:02,099 --> 00:04:05,834
Divide each by root two to make them unit vectors.

67
00:04:06,234 --> 00:04:09,647
Notice that these two directions are already perpendicular.

68
00:04:09,667 --> 00:04:13,197
We did nothing to make them so. That is the gift of symmetry.

69
00:04:13,597 --> 00:04:18,223
Step three, put the unit eigenvectors into the columns of Q,

70
00:04:18,243 --> 00:04:23,721
put the eigenvalues in the same order into Λ, and A is written as QΛQᵀ.

71
00:04:24,121 --> 00:04:28,028
Finally, check. The dot product is zero, both lengths are one,

72
00:04:28,048 --> 00:04:31,068
and multiplying the three matrices back gives A.

73
00:04:31,088 --> 00:04:34,551
You are about to see this very matrix in the animation.

74
00:04:36,071 --> 00:04:41,120
So what does A = QΛQᵀ mean geometrically? Let's watch.

75
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:46,931
Here A is the 2 × 2 symmetric matrix (2 1; 1 2). Its eigenvalues are 1 and 3,

76
00:04:46,951 --> 00:04:50,669
and the green and red eigenvectors are perpendicular.

77
00:04:51,069 --> 00:04:53,091
Step one. Qᵀ is a rotation.

78
00:04:53,111 --> 00:04:57,249
It turns the two eigenvectors onto the coordinate axes.

79
00:04:57,269 --> 00:05:00,273
Q can always be chosen to be a rotation.

80
00:05:00,672 --> 00:05:03,308
Step two. Λ stretches along the axes,

81
00:05:03,328 --> 00:05:06,681
by a factor of 1 horizontally and 3 vertically.

82
00:05:06,701 --> 00:05:09,193
The two directions don't interfere.

83
00:05:09,593 --> 00:05:11,419
Step three. Q rotates back.

84
00:05:11,439 --> 00:05:16,616
The unit circle has become an ellipse whose axes lie along the eigenvectors.

85
00:05:17,016 --> 00:05:19,736
Finally, transform the circle by A directly.

86
00:05:19,756 --> 00:05:23,659
The white curve lands exactly on the result of the three steps.

87
00:05:24,059 --> 00:05:25,072
In one sentence:

88
00:05:25,092 --> 00:05:30,239
a symmetric matrix stretches space independently along perpendicular directions.

89
00:05:30,259 --> 00:05:35,148
The circle becomes an ellipse whose semi-axes are the absolute values of the

90
00:05:35,168 --> 00:05:35,923
eigenvalues.

91
00:05:37,443 --> 00:05:43,575
Multiply out QΛQᵀ as columns times rows, and you get another way to write it.

92
00:05:43,975 --> 00:05:52,630
A = λ₁q₁q₁ᵀ + λ₂q₂q₂ᵀ + … + λₙqₙqₙᵀ.

93
00:05:53,030 --> 00:05:56,686
Each qᵢqᵢᵀ is the projection onto the i-th axis.

94
00:05:56,706 --> 00:05:59,826
These projections add up to the identity,

95
00:05:59,846 --> 00:06:03,196
and any two different ones multiply to zero.

96
00:06:03,596 --> 00:06:07,968
So a symmetric matrix is a weighted sum of perpendicular projections,

97
00:06:07,988 --> 00:06:11,596
and the weights are the eigenvalues. Applied to a vector,

98
00:06:11,616 --> 00:06:16,305
it projects onto each axis, scales by the eigenvalue, and adds the pieces.

99
00:06:16,325 --> 00:06:18,533
This is the spectral decomposition.

100
00:06:18,933 --> 00:06:22,706
It makes functions of a matrix easy. To get f(A),

101
00:06:22,726 --> 00:06:25,725
just replace each eigenvalue λ by f(λ).

102
00:06:26,125 --> 00:06:30,907
For example, the compliant wrist has stiffness K equal to (4 1; 1 4),

103
00:06:30,927 --> 00:06:32,647
with eigenvalues 3 and 5.

104
00:06:32,667 --> 00:06:36,822
Replace them by √3 and √5 and you have the square root of K.

105
00:06:36,842 --> 00:06:41,833
Square roots of symmetric positive definite matrices show up in weighted

106
00:06:41,853 --> 00:06:44,338
least squares and in Kalman filters.

107
00:06:45,858 --> 00:06:48,079
Now a 3 by 3 example.

108
00:06:48,479 --> 00:06:53,056
This matrix S has 2 on the diagonal and minus 1 next to it.

109
00:06:53,076 --> 00:06:58,822
It shows up in spring chains, finite differences and spline interpolation,

110
00:06:58,842 --> 00:07:02,406
and its eigenvalues are 2 − √2, 2, and 2 + √2.

111
00:07:02,806 --> 00:07:07,550
The unit eigenvector for the eigenvalue 2 is (1, 0, −1)/√2.

112
00:07:07,570 --> 00:07:13,606
Multiplying it by its own transpose gives the projection P₂ onto that axis.

113
00:07:14,006 --> 00:07:18,566
The program adds the three projections and gets the identity up to about ten

114
00:07:18,586 --> 00:07:19,832
to the minus sixteen.

115
00:07:19,852 --> 00:07:22,846
Weight them by the eigenvalues and you get S back.

116
00:07:22,866 --> 00:07:27,487
That is the spectral decomposition on a computer: the only error is rounding.

117
00:07:29,007 --> 00:07:36,134
Back to the robot. A planar two-link arm has links of 0.4 and 0.3 metres,

118
00:07:36,154 --> 00:07:40,735
and joint stiffnesses of 2000 and 1000 N·m/rad.

119
00:07:41,135 --> 00:07:47,051
The tip compliance is C = JK_q⁻¹Jᵀ, and the tip stiffness is its inverse.

120
00:07:47,071 --> 00:07:52,173
At the pose (0°, 90°), the program gives this stiffness matrix,

121
00:07:52,193 --> 00:07:53,799
and it is symmetric.

122
00:07:54,199 --> 00:08:00,163
Run eigh, and the principal stiffnesses come out as 5764 and 24097 N/m,

123
00:08:00,183 --> 00:08:05,556
more than four times apart. The soft axis points at 147 degrees,

124
00:08:05,576 --> 00:08:10,275
the stiff axis at 57 degrees, perpendicular as promised.

125
00:08:10,675 --> 00:08:12,944
Now push straight down with 10 newtons.

126
00:08:12,964 --> 00:08:15,879
The spectral decomposition does it in three steps.

127
00:08:16,279 --> 00:08:19,773
Step one, split the force onto the two axes.

128
00:08:19,793 --> 00:08:25,043
About 5.4 newtons land on the soft axis and 8.4 on the stiff axis.

129
00:08:25,443 --> 00:08:28,658
Step two, divide each by its stiffness.

130
00:08:28,678 --> 00:08:34,382
The soft axis moves about 0.94 millimetres, the stiff axis only 0.35.

131
00:08:34,782 --> 00:08:40,094
Step three, add them back. The deflection is (0.6, −0.8) millimetres,

132
00:08:40,114 --> 00:08:43,185
the same as solving the system directly.

133
00:08:43,585 --> 00:08:46,001
Notice that the push is straight down,

134
00:08:46,021 --> 00:08:49,719
yet the deflection is about 37 degrees away from the push,

135
00:08:49,739 --> 00:08:51,065
toward the soft axis.

136
00:08:51,085 --> 00:08:55,360
Push with 10 newtons in every direction and the tips trace out this

137
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:56,578
compliance ellipse.

138
00:08:58,098 --> 00:09:01,112
All these numbers come from Program 14.2.1.

139
00:09:01,132 --> 00:09:04,639
Every number in the book is computed by a program.

140
00:09:05,039 --> 00:09:08,186
The heart of it is a few lines. Build the compliance,

141
00:09:08,206 --> 00:09:12,667
invert it to get the stiffness, call eigh, project the force onto the axes,

142
00:09:12,687 --> 00:09:15,237
divide by the stiffnesses, and rotate back.

143
00:09:15,637 --> 00:09:19,138
Here is the output. You can run it on the platform yourself,

144
00:09:19,158 --> 00:09:21,427
change the pose or the joint stiffness,

145
00:09:21,447 --> 00:09:23,950
and watch the principal stiffnesses change.

146
00:09:25,471 --> 00:09:27,853
Three things to watch when you write the code.

147
00:09:28,253 --> 00:09:32,109
First, the signs of the eigenvectors from eigh are arbitrary.

148
00:09:32,129 --> 00:09:35,732
When you plot axes or compare poses, fix the signs first,

149
00:09:35,752 --> 00:09:40,943
or an axis will suddenly flip by a hundred and eighty degrees and look like a bug.

150
00:09:41,343 --> 00:09:41,740
Second,

151
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:46,555
a matrix that should be symmetric is often off by about ten to the minus sixteen,

152
00:09:46,575 --> 00:09:51,014
for instance when two off-diagonal entries are summed in a different order.

153
00:09:51,034 --> 00:09:53,986
Average A with its transpose before you call eigh.

154
00:09:54,386 --> 00:09:54,778
Third,

155
00:09:54,798 --> 00:09:59,723
computing a matrix function through the spectral decomposition works for

156
00:09:59,743 --> 00:10:01,027
symmetric matrices.

157
00:10:01,047 --> 00:10:04,942
For a general matrix, use scipy's sqrtm and expm instead.

158
00:10:06,462 --> 00:10:08,779
The spectral theorem is all around you.

159
00:10:09,179 --> 00:10:12,035
Take a square rubber sheet and draw a circle on it.

160
00:10:12,055 --> 00:10:15,701
Stretch it east–west to twice its length and north–south by half.

161
00:10:15,721 --> 00:10:17,393
The circle becomes an ellipse,

162
00:10:17,413 --> 00:10:21,116
and its long and short axes are exactly the directions you pulled.

163
00:10:21,516 --> 00:10:24,429
Look closely: every other line you drew turns a little.

164
00:10:24,829 --> 00:10:29,424
Any pure stretch with no rotation behaves this way. Its matrix is symmetric,

165
00:10:29,444 --> 00:10:32,096
the pulling directions are the eigenvectors,

166
00:10:32,116 --> 00:10:34,768
and the stretch factors are the eigenvalues.

167
00:10:36,288 --> 00:10:41,252
Now let's open Lab 14.2, the stiffness ellipse at a robot's tip.

168
00:10:41,652 --> 00:10:44,698
Here is a planar two-link arm with elastic joints.

169
00:10:44,718 --> 00:10:47,948
The ellipse around the tip is the compliance ellipse.

170
00:10:47,968 --> 00:10:52,118
Its long axis is the soft axis and its short axis is the stiff axis.

171
00:10:52,518 --> 00:10:55,693
Drag the push direction φ. Except along the two axes,

172
00:10:55,713 --> 00:11:00,576
the deflection always swerves toward the soft axis instead of following the push.

173
00:11:00,976 --> 00:11:04,211
The readout on the right shows the angle between them.

174
00:11:04,231 --> 00:11:07,466
It is zero only when the push lies exactly on an axis.

175
00:11:07,486 --> 00:11:11,805
Your first task is to turn the push onto the softest direction yourself,

176
00:11:11,825 --> 00:11:15,904
so that the deflection lines up with it and is as large as possible.

177
00:11:16,304 --> 00:11:20,491
Now turn the first joint, and keep an eye on the two principal stiffnesses.

178
00:11:20,891 --> 00:11:25,495
The axes turn with the arm, but the stiffness values do not change. Why not?

179
00:11:25,515 --> 00:11:28,416
That is the question to think about in this lab.

180
00:11:28,816 --> 00:11:31,139
Now change the angle of the second joint.

181
00:11:31,159 --> 00:11:33,711
Make it smaller and the ellipse gets flatter.

182
00:11:34,111 --> 00:11:36,303
Make it larger and it gets rounder. So,

183
00:11:36,323 --> 00:11:40,047
can you make the tip equally stiff in every direction? Which pose,

184
00:11:40,067 --> 00:11:43,735
which stiffness? Each of the four tasks is checked automatically,

185
00:11:43,755 --> 00:11:46,911
and the lab guide and report template are ready for you.

186
00:11:48,431 --> 00:11:52,682
What you learned today is used in the WenQuest digital factory as well.

187
00:11:53,082 --> 00:11:55,756
This is the motion and dynamics module.

188
00:11:55,776 --> 00:12:00,247
It uses MuJoCo to simulate a UR5e arm carrying a 3 kilogram load,

189
00:12:00,267 --> 00:12:02,458
plots the torque at every joint,

190
00:12:02,478 --> 00:12:06,189
and then picks the motors and gearboxes automatically.

191
00:12:06,589 --> 00:12:08,060
A gearbox is not rigid.

192
00:12:08,080 --> 00:12:12,599
Its torsional stiffness is the main source of the joint stiffness k in

193
00:12:12,619 --> 00:12:13,636
today's formula.

194
00:12:14,036 --> 00:12:16,018
Once the gearbox is chosen,

195
00:12:16,038 --> 00:12:20,022
you can plug its stiffness into C = JK_q⁻¹Jᵀ yourself,

196
00:12:20,042 --> 00:12:24,026
get the arm's stiffness ellipsoid in its working pose,

197
00:12:24,046 --> 00:12:27,140
and see which way the tool will be pushed.

198
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:33,319
On the platform you can try today's method on the digital factory's UR5e simulation.

199
00:12:34,839 --> 00:12:37,348
There are four exercises for this lecture.

200
00:12:37,748 --> 00:12:43,277
The first is a computation. Write a 2 × 2 symmetric matrix as QΛQᵀ,

201
00:12:43,297 --> 00:12:45,927
and as a spectral decomposition.

202
00:12:46,327 --> 00:12:50,782
The second is a proof. If a symmetric matrix satisfies A² = A,

203
00:12:50,802 --> 00:12:53,380
show it is an orthogonal projection.

204
00:12:53,780 --> 00:12:55,291
The third is programming.

205
00:12:55,311 --> 00:13:00,434
Compute a matrix exponential from the spectral decomposition and compare with scipy.

206
00:13:00,834 --> 00:13:02,637
The fourth is about the robot.

207
00:13:02,657 --> 00:13:06,042
Find the principal stiffnesses and axes at another pose,

208
00:13:06,062 --> 00:13:09,811
and explain why turning the first joint leaves them unchanged.

209
00:13:10,211 --> 00:13:12,119
Plus the report for Lab 14.2.

210
00:13:12,139 --> 00:13:17,038
Submit your work on the platform and the AI gives you feedback right away,

211
00:13:17,058 --> 00:13:17,902
step by step.

212
00:13:19,422 --> 00:13:21,504
To sum up in three sentences.

213
00:13:21,524 --> 00:13:26,649
A real symmetric matrix is exactly a matrix you can diagonalize with an

214
00:13:26,669 --> 00:13:27,954
orthogonal matrix.

215
00:13:28,354 --> 00:13:32,382
Geometrically, it rotates, stretches along perpendicular axes,

216
00:13:32,402 --> 00:13:33,493
and rotates back.

217
00:13:33,893 --> 00:13:37,969
The spectral decomposition writes it as a weighted sum of projections,

218
00:13:37,989 --> 00:13:42,124
and the axes of a robot's stiffness ellipse are the eigenvectors of its

219
00:13:42,144 --> 00:13:43,119
stiffness matrix.

220
00:13:43,519 --> 00:13:44,965
The lab, the program,

221
00:13:44,985 --> 00:13:49,991
the notes and the exercises are all on the WenQuest learning platform at

222
00:13:50,011 --> 00:13:51,875
learn.wenquestrobotics.com.

223
00:13:51,895 --> 00:13:54,597
Sign up for free and try them yourself.

224
00:13:54,997 --> 00:13:58,939
Next time we extend the spectral theorem to complex matrices,

225
00:13:58,959 --> 00:14:04,004
with Hermitian and unitary matrices and the Schur decomposition. See you then.
